Article 10 In any of the following circumstances, the institute of certified public accountants that accepts the application shall not grant registration:
(1) The applicant does not have full capacity for civil conduct;
(2) The applicant has been subjected to criminal punishment, and less than five years have elapsed from the date of completion of the execution of the punishment to the date of application for registration;
(3) The applicant has committed serious errors in financial, accounting, audit, enterprise management, or other economic management work and has been subjected to administrative penalty or sanction of removal from office or above, and less than two years have elapsed from the date of the decision on penalty or sanction to the date of application for registration;
(4) The applicant has been subjected to the penalty of having his or her certified public accountant certificate revoked, and less than five years have elapsed from the date of the decision on penalty to the date of application for registration;
(5) Other circumstances under which registration shall not be granted as prescribed by the finance department of the State Council.

Article 13 For a person who has obtained a certified public accountant certificate, if any of the following circumstances occurs after registration, except the circumstances specified in the first paragraph of Article 11 of this Law, the institute of certified public accountants that granted the registration shall revoke the registration and withdraw the certified public accountant certificate:
(1) The person has completely lost capacity for civil conduct;
(2) The person has been subjected to criminal punishment;
(3) The person has committed serious errors in financial, accounting, audit, enterprise management, or other economic management work and has been subjected to administrative penalty or sanction of removal from office or above;
(4) The person has voluntarily ceased to practice as a certified public accountant for a full year.
If the party whose registration has been revoked has objections, he or she may, within fifteen days from receipt of the notice of revocation of registration and withdrawal of the certified public accountant certificate, apply to the finance department of the State Council or the finance department of the people's government of the relevant province, autonomous region, or municipality directly under the Central Government for reconsideration.
A person whose registration has been revoked in accordance with the first paragraph may apply for registration again, but must meet the requirements of Articles 9 and 10 of this Law.

Article 14 Certified public accountants shall undertake the following audit services:
(1) Examining the financial statements of enterprises and issuing audit reports;
(2) Verifying the capital of enterprises and issuing capital verification reports;
(3) Handling audit services in connection with merger, division, and liquidation of enterprises and issuing relevant reports;
(4) Other audit services as prescribed by laws and administrative regulations.
The reports issued by certified public accountants when performing audit services according to law shall have evidentiary effect.

Article 20 When performing audit services, a certified public accountant shall refuse to issue a relevant report under any of the following circumstances:
(1) The client suggests that the certified public accountant make a false or improper certification;
(2) The client intentionally fails to provide relevant accounting materials and documents;
(3) Due to other unreasonable requirements of the client, the report to be issued by the certified public accountant cannot give a correct description of the important matters of financial accounting.

Article 21 When performing audit services, a certified public accountant must issue a report in accordance with the working procedures determined by the professional standards and rules.
When issuing a report on performing audit services, a certified public accountant shall not engage in any of the following acts:
(1) failing to point out that the financial accounting treatment of the client on material matters is inconsistent with the relevant provisions of the State, when knowing of such inconsistency;
(2) concealing or making a false report on the financial accounting treatment of the client that directly damages the interests of the users of the report or other interested parties, when knowing that such damage will occur;
(3) failing to point out that the financial accounting treatment of the client causes material misunderstanding among the users of the report or other interested parties, when knowing that such misunderstanding will occur;
(4) failing to point out that there are other false contents in the material matters of the client's accounting statements, when knowing of such false contents.
Where the client commits any of the acts listed in the preceding paragraph which a certified public accountant ought to know in accordance with the professional standards and rules, the provisions of the preceding paragraph shall apply.

Article 22 A certified public accountant shall not engage in any of the following acts:
(1) during the period of performing audit services, buying or selling the stocks or bonds of the audited entity or purchasing other property owned by the audited entity or individual within the period during which the buying or selling of such stocks or bonds or the purchase of such property is prohibited by laws or administrative regulations;
(2) demanding or accepting remuneration or other property other than as agreed in the entrustment contract, or seeking other illegitimate benefits by taking advantage of the convenience of performing the business;
(3) accepting an entrustment to collect debts;
(4) allowing others to perform business in his/her own name;
(5) simultaneously performing business in two or more accounting firms;
(6) advertising his/her capabilities to solicit business;
(7) other acts that violate laws or administrative regulations.

Article 24 An accounting firm that meets the following conditions may be a legal person with limited liability:
(1) having a registered capital of not less than 300,000 yuan;
(2) having a certain number of full-time employees, among whom there are at least five certified public accountants;
(3) having the scope of business and other conditions as prescribed by the finance department of the State Council.
An accounting firm with limited liability shall bear liability for its debts with all its assets.

Article 25 The establishment of an accounting firm shall be subject to the approval of the finance department of the people's government of the province, autonomous region, or municipality directly under the Central Government.
When applying for the establishment of an accounting firm, the applicant shall submit the following documents to the approving authority:
(1) a written application;
(2) the name, organizational structure, and business premises of the accounting firm;
(3) the articles of association of the accounting firm, and if there is a partnership agreement, the partnership agreement shall also be submitted;
(4) a list of certified public accountants, their resumes, and relevant certification documents;
(5) the names and resumes of the principal persons in charge and partners of the accounting firm, and relevant certification documents;
(6) the capital contribution certificate of the accounting firm with limited liability;
(7) other documents as required by the approving authority.
